course: everyday
lesson: 385
title: Respect: how it is earned
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Jake had always thought respect came from your fists. Then he went in goal for the Year 3s' lunchtime match, dived full length in the mud and saved a penalty. The little ones mobbed him: «Jake! Jake! Best goalie ever!» They had always kept away from him in the playground. Now they waved at him every morning. Jake realised they had been scared of him before — and that scared is not the same as respect.
explain: Respect turned up in other places too. Maddie won the school cross-country by half a field, and the whole school cheered her, not because she was tough but because she had trained every morning for weeks. Sam, who hates talking in front of people, spoke up in assembly about the litter in the park, and his voice shook the whole time — afterwards the class looked at him differently. Lily never says a mean word about anyone, so when she says something, people listen. Even Jake admitted: «I respect Sam. He never pretends to be hard.»
explain: Kieran still thought being the hardest kid on the street earned respect. But people only did what he said when he was standing there, and laughed at him when he left. Connor learned the hard way: after he punched Maddie's nose and said it was «all for equality», his own friends stopped sticking up for him — the excuse made it worse, not better. And Chloe? She respects Maddie a bit for the cross-country, but she still stays away from her, because she remembers the fights. Respect, once lost, comes back slowly.
explain: Jake's uncle, who boxes at a club, said the wisest thing of all: «The best boxers are the ones who never fight outside the ring. Strength you control earns respect. Strength you throw around just earns fear.» Respect comes from skill, effort, honesty, kindness and courage — not from making people afraid.
fact: Respect means people value and trust you; fear means people avoid you or obey you only while you are there.
fact: Respect is earned by effort, skill, honesty, kindness and courage.
fact: Fighting may make people afraid of you, but fear is not the same as respect.
fact: Speaking up for something good when you are nervous is a kind of courage.
fact: People who never say mean things about others are trusted more, because they will not talk about you behind your back either.
fact: Admitting you are not tough, or that you were wrong, often earns more respect than pretending.
fact: An excuse for hurting someone usually makes people think worse of you, not better.
fact: Respect that has been lost can come back, but only slowly, through steady good behaviour.
fact: In boxing clubs, coaches teach that skills are only for the ring, with rules and a referee, never for the street.
fact: Someone can respect you a little and still keep their distance; trust takes longer than respect.
fact: Hard work you can see, like training for a race, earns respect from many people at once.
exercise: Why did the Year 3s used to keep away from Jake? | They were scared of him | They thought he was too quiet | He was too young to play with | He never came outside to play
exercise: What earned Jake real respect from the Year 3s? | Saving a penalty in goal for them | Winning a fight by the bins after school | Shouting the loudest | Taking their football
exercise: What did Jake realise about fear and respect? | Being feared is not the same as being respected | Fear and respect are really the same thing | Only fear lasts | Respect comes only from fighting someone
exercise: Why did the whole school cheer Maddie at the cross-country? | She had trained hard and run brilliantly | She had beaten someone up the week before | She was the tallest runner in the whole race | She had pushed people over at the start to win it
exercise: Why did the class respect Sam after the assembly? | He was brave enough to speak up though he was nervous | He spoke very loudly and never stopped | He told funny jokes about the teacher all the way through | He refused to talk at all
exercise: Why do people listen to Lily? | She never says mean things about anyone | She is the loudest girl in the whole class | She always gets her own way | She tells everyone's secrets
exercise: Why does Jake respect Sam? | Sam never pretends to be tough | Sam can beat him at arm wrestling | Sam lets Jake copy his homework | Sam is the hardest boy in the street
exercise: What really happened when Kieran acted «hard»? | People obeyed him only when he was there | Everyone trusted him more than before | People asked him for advice | The teachers made him the football captain
exercise: Why did Connor's friends lose respect for him? | He hurt Maddie and then made a poor excuse | He lost an important football match | He would not share his sweets with anyone at all | He moved to another school
exercise: Did Connor's «equality» excuse help? | No, it made things worse | Yes, it made everything fine | Yes, because it was clever | Only with the teachers, not the kids
exercise: Chloe respects Maddie a bit but stays away. Why? | She still remembers Maddie's fights | She is jealous of the medal Maddie won | She lives on Station Road with the other girls | Maddie is rude to her every single day
exercise: What did Jake's uncle mean about the best boxers? | Skill used with control earns respect; fighting in the street earns fear | Boxers should fight anyone who annoys them, inside the ring or out | Boxers never get hurt in the ring | Boxing is only about winning
exercise: Why do boxing clubs keep fighting inside the ring? | There are rules, a referee and protection there | It is warmer inside the ring than out | The street is too small | Coaches want to watch every single punch closely
exercise: How can lost respect come back? | Slowly, through steady good behaviour | All at once, with one big apology in front of everyone | By winning a fight | It can never come back at all
exercise: Which of these earns respect? | Owning up when you were wrong | Blaming someone smaller than you | Pretending you are hard all the time | Laughing at people who fall over
exercise: Which is the difference between respect and fear? | Respect lasts when you leave; fear goes when you leave | Fear lasts much longer than respect does, so it's better | They are exactly the same | Respect only comes from adults
test: Kieran: «everyone respects me cos I'm the hardest on the street». Sam's wise reply? | They're scared of you, mate — that's not respect | Yeah you're well respected, mate, everyone thinks so | Then fight me to prove it | I respect you most of all
test: «just batter someone in Year 6, then nobody'll mess with u». Is that how respect works? | No, it just makes people scared and keeps away | Yes, it always works, people do whatever you say after | Only if they're bigger | Only if you win
test: Little kid at the gate: «Jake! Are you in goal again Friday?» What earned Jake that? | Playing well and being kind to them | Fighting their big brothers to show them who's boss | Taking their crisps | Being loud in the dinner hall
test: Sam, after assembly: «my voice was all shaky, so embarrassing». His mate says? | It was brave — you did it anyway | Yeah, everyone laughed at you though | You should've just not bothered | Next time shout it
test: Connor: «I only hit her cos it's all for equality innit». Why do his mates go quiet? | Hurting someone and making excuses loses respect | They all agree with him | They didn't hear him | They think it's funny that he did it and got away with it
test: «Lily's boring, she never slags anyone off». What's the real reason people trust Lily? | She won't talk about you behind your back either | She's got nothing to say about anything | She's scared of everyone in the class, even the teacher | She's only nice to teachers
test: Uncle at the boxing club: «never in the street, only in here». What's the reason? | In the ring there are rules and a ref; in the street people get badly hurt | Street fights are too easy for a real boxer to bother with | You don't get paid in the street | The street is too cold to fight in
test: Chloe: «yeah she won the run, well good, but I'm still not sitting with her». Is that fair? | Yes, trust takes longer to come back than respect | No, she has to sit with her, it's on the seating plan | No, Chloe is just jealous | Yes, because girls who run are odd
test: Jake to Sam: «tbh I respect u, u never act hard». What does this show? | Being honest about yourself earns respect | Jake is making fun of Sam for being soft | Sam should start acting hard like the others do | Respect only comes from fighting
test: Teacher: «Maddie, what got you that medal?» Best answer? | Getting up early and training for weeks | Being scarier than the other runners at the start | Elbowing people at the start | Luck, nothing else
test: Kieran wants real respect. What should he try? | Doing something well and being decent to people | Starting a bigger fight with somebody tougher | Getting a scarier gang name than anyone else | Making the little ones pay him
exam: Identify the principal distinction between respect and fear as described. | Respect endures in a person's absence, whereas fear secures obedience only in his presence | Fear is a more durable form of respect | Respect and fear are indistinguishable in practice, both securing obedience at all times | Fear is earned by effort and respect by force
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the means by which respect is earned? | Through effort, skill, honesty, kindness and courage | Through the demonstration of physical dominance over others | Through the intimidation of younger pupils | Through the avoidance of all public activity
exam: It is given that a pupil, having struck another, offers a justification in terms of «equality». Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The justification tends to diminish further the respect of his peers | The justification restores fully the respect of his peers | The justification renders the act praiseworthy | The justification is without any effect upon his peers
exam: State the principle conveyed by the observation that the best boxers do not fight outside the ring. | Controlled strength confined to a regulated setting earns respect | Strength ought to be exercised wherever provocation arises | Boxers are prohibited from all physical activity | The ring is the sole place in which a person may lawfully be injured
